WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.540
Hey, welcome back.

00:00:02.280 --> 00:00:06.511
A battery labelled twelve volts does not always deliver twelve volts across

00:00:06.511 --> 00:00:06.680
the

00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:06.740
load.

00:00:07.480 --> 00:00:08.300
Let's explain why.

00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:13.880
Model the cell as an ideal emf E in series with internal resistance r.

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:18.420
Emf tells us how much energy the source mechanism supplies per unit charge.

00:00:19.280 --> 00:00:22.860
Some of that energy becomes heat inside the cell, leaving less potential

00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:23.860
difference at the terminals.

00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:29.860
Our example has emf twelve volts, internal resistance two ohms and external

00:00:29.860 --> 00:00:31.040
resistance four ohms.

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:35.260
Total resistance is six ohms, so current is two amperes.

00:00:36.020 --> 00:00:39.900
The internal voltage drop is current times internal resistance, four volts.

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:42.720
Terminal voltage is therefore eight volts.

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:46.080
It also equals current times the external resistance.

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:50.000
Now check power: the source supplies twenty four watts.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:54.440
The load receives sixteen watts, and internal heating takes eight watts.

00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:55.940
They add correctly.

00:00:56.960 --> 00:01:00.940
This is a constant parameter model, not a complete description of a real cell

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:02.660
as it heats or discharges.

00:01:03.740 --> 00:01:04.880
Now let's change the load.

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:08.980
Would making it very small always give the largest useful power?

00:01:09.820 --> 00:01:10.600
Think about that.

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:14.180
A tiny load draws current, but has little voltage across it.

00:01:14.180 --> 00:01:17.560
A very large load has voltage but little current.

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:23.080
Load power is E squared times R divided by the square of R plus r.

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:30.045
Differentiating with respect to R gives E squared times r minus R, divided by

00:01:30.045 --> 00:01:30.120
R

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:31.280
plus r cubed.

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:34.360
It changes from positive to negative when R equals r.

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:39.020
So our twelve volt, two ohm source gives maximum load power with a two ohm

00:01:39.020 --> 00:01:40.200
load: eighteen watts.

00:01:40.820 --> 00:01:44.160
But the internal resistance also dissipates eighteen watts.

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:46.320
Efficiency is only fifty percent.

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:49.580
Maximum power and maximum efficiency are different goals.

00:01:50.500 --> 00:01:54.080
With a larger load, the fraction of source power delivered outside improves,

00:01:54.580 --> 00:01:55.760
but delivered power falls.

00:01:56.700 --> 00:01:59.840
For JEE, read which objective the question asks for.

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:04.680
And never treat the ideal maximum power calculation as advice to short a real

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:04.840
battery.

00:02:05.304 --> 00:02:09.284
Next, a question that sounds simple but hides an important condition.

00:02:10.124 --> 00:02:13.124
A metal resistor warms up and its resistance increases.

00:02:14.004 --> 00:02:16.464
Does its electrical power increase or decrease?

00:02:17.524 --> 00:02:20.364
Before choosing, ask what the source holds constant.

00:02:21.364 --> 00:02:25.984
Close to a reference temperature, resistance is approximately R zero times one

00:02:25.984 --> 00:02:27.864
plus alpha times the temperature change.

00:02:28.684 --> 00:02:30.424
Alpha is defined at that reference temperature.

00:02:30.864 --> 00:02:35.624
This is a local linear approximation, not a universal rule for every material

00:02:35.624 --> 00:02:36.384
or temperature.

00:02:37.284 --> 00:02:41.397
Suppose resistance is ten ohms at twenty degrees Celsius, with alpha zero

00:02:41.397 --> 00:02:41.679
point

00:02:41.679 --> 00:02:42.964
zero zero four per kelvin.

00:02:43.924 --> 00:02:45.344
Warm it to seventy degrees Celsius.

00:02:45.924 --> 00:02:49.844
The temperature change is fifty degrees, numerically the same as fifty kelvin.

00:02:50.624 --> 00:02:53.624
Resistance becomes ten times one point two, or twelve ohms.

00:02:54.404 --> 00:02:56.864
Now use an ideal constant twelve volt supply.

00:02:57.564 --> 00:03:01.824
Initially current is one point two amperes and power is fourteen point four

00:03:01.824 --> 00:03:02.084
watts.

00:03:02.704 --> 00:03:06.484
At twelve ohms, current is one ampere and power is twelve watts.

00:03:07.404 --> 00:03:11.244
Power decreased because voltage stayed fixed and power is voltage squared

00:03:11.244 --> 00:03:12.284
divided by resistance.

00:03:13.284 --> 00:03:14.284
Try the other constraint.

00:03:15.224 --> 00:03:18.044
Suppose an ideal source holds current at one point two amperes.

00:03:18.624 --> 00:03:21.044
At ten ohms, power is fourteen point four watts.

00:03:21.724 --> 00:03:24.364
At twelve ohms it becomes seventeen point two eight watts.

00:03:25.044 --> 00:03:28.864
It increased because current squared times resistance is now the relevant

00:03:28.864 --> 00:03:29.304
expression.

00:03:30.024 --> 00:03:32.564
Same resistor change, opposite power trend.

00:03:33.324 --> 00:03:36.344
So do not memorize that heating always reduces electrical power.

00:03:37.244 --> 00:03:38.484
State the source constraint first.

00:03:39.244 --> 00:03:42.904
Real circuits can also include internal source resistance, and self heating

00:03:42.904 --> 00:03:45.384
needs a heat loss model if temperature is not prescribed.

00:03:46.244 --> 00:03:50.164
For our numerical question, geometry changes are neglected and the given

00:03:50.164 --> 00:03:51.244
temperature is an input.

00:03:51.924 --> 00:03:55.783
Our routine is to calculate resistance using a temperature difference,

00:03:55.783 --> 00:03:56.224
identify

00:03:56.224 --> 00:03:59.344
the source, and only then calculate current and power.

00:04:00.264 --> 00:04:02.964
Let's finish with a bridge that is not balanced.

00:04:03.584 --> 00:04:05.044
Keep the diagram beside you.

00:04:05.544 --> 00:04:08.624
Supply node A is twelve volts and node D is zero.

00:04:09.484 --> 00:04:13.849
The upper path goes from A through three ohms to B, then through six ohms to

00:04:13.849 --> 00:04:13.964
D.

00:04:14.524 --> 00:04:18.714
The lower path goes from A through six ohms to C, then through three ohms to

00:04:18.714 --> 00:04:18.824
D.

00:04:19.384 --> 00:04:21.464
A three ohm bridge joins B to C.

00:04:22.164 --> 00:04:25.464
Can we treat the upper and lower paths as independent voltage dividers?

00:04:25.944 --> 00:04:27.624
Not while bridge current flows.

00:04:28.044 --> 00:04:31.244
The middle resistor changes both node potentials.

00:04:31.444 --> 00:04:36.144
At B, write outgoing currents: B minus twelve divided by three, plus B divided

00:04:36.144 --> 00:04:38.884
by six, plus B minus C divided by three.

00:04:39.384 --> 00:04:40.244
Their sum is zero.

00:04:41.024 --> 00:04:45.304
At C, the outgoing sum is C minus twelve divided by six, plus C divided by

00:04:45.304 --> 00:04:47.804
three, plus C minus B divided by three.

00:04:49.084 --> 00:04:50.544
Multiply both equations by six.

00:04:50.964 --> 00:04:55.744
We get five B minus two C equals twenty four, and minus two B plus five C

00:04:55.744 --> 00:04:56.544
equals twelve.

00:04:56.544 --> 00:05:01.384
Solve them together: B is forty eight sevenths volts, and C is thirty six

00:05:01.384 --> 00:05:02.084
sevenths volts.

00:05:02.984 --> 00:05:05.744
B is higher, so bridge current goes from B to C.

00:05:06.164 --> 00:05:09.884
Its value is the difference divided by three ohms: four sevenths of an ampere.

00:05:10.444 --> 00:05:13.844
A negative current under an assumed arrow would simply tell us the real

00:05:13.844 --> 00:05:14.744
direction is opposite.

00:05:15.364 --> 00:05:16.024
Now verify.

00:05:16.764 --> 00:05:20.084
At B, incoming current from A is twelve sevenths of an ampere.

00:05:20.804 --> 00:05:24.524
Outgoing current to D is eight sevenths, and to C four sevenths.

00:05:24.524 --> 00:05:26.284
Charge conservation checks.

00:05:27.064 --> 00:05:30.364
Supply current is twenty sevenths of an ampere, giving equivalent resistance

00:05:30.364 --> 00:05:31.524
four point two ohms.

00:05:32.504 --> 00:05:36.124
Removing the middle branch gives two nine ohm paths in parallel, or four point

00:05:36.124 --> 00:05:36.904
five ohms.

00:05:37.724 --> 00:05:40.224
Adding a conducting path reduced resistance, as expected.

00:05:41.504 --> 00:05:45.554
Finally, source power is twelve times twenty sevenths, or two hundred forty

00:05:45.554 --> 00:05:45.904
sevenths watts.

00:05:46.744 --> 00:05:50.944
Adding current squared times resistance over all five resistors gives the same

00:05:50.944 --> 00:05:51.244
result.

00:05:51.464 --> 00:05:53.084
That is a strong check.

00:05:53.084 --> 00:05:58.284
Draw nodes, write signs consistently, solve independent equations, then verify

00:05:58.284 --> 00:05:59.124
charge and energy.

00:05:59.764 --> 00:06:02.904
A circuit's appearance is not enough to decide series or parallel.
